    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n46640" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἤγουν</span></b> , Conj., (<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ἤ γε
		οὖν</span>) <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>A.</b><i>that is to say,
				or rather</i>, to define a word more correctly, freq. in
			glosses, cf. <i><span class="en">
				Eust.
				50.15
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				Lyd.
				<u>Mens.</u>
				4.23
			</span></i>, etc.: sts. introduced into the text, 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">κακὰ πάντα [ἤγουν τήν τε ἀπεψίην]
					καί . . </span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					Hp.
					<u>Acut.</u>
				</span></i>
			(<u>Sp.</u>)<i><span class="en">
				49
			</span></i> (<i><span class="en">
				ii 491
			</span></i> L.); 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">διὰ ξηρότητα [ἤγουν χαυνότητα] τῆς
					γῆς</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">
					X.
					<u>Oec.</u>
					19.11
				</span></i>
			: in late Prose, <i>or at any rate,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				PMasp.
				328 i 20
			</span></i>
			<span class="entity">(vi A.D.)</span>, al.: generally, <i>or,</i>
			<i><span class="en">
				POxy.
				941.5
			</span></i>
			<span class="entity">(vi A.D.)</span>.</div>
